1. What is a Constructor?
A constructor is a special method used to initialize objects.

Key Characteristics
  Name must be same as class name
  No return type (not even void)
  Can be overloaded
  Cannot be static, final, abstract, or synchronized
  Executed only once per object creation

2. Why Do We Need Constructors?
| Purpose            | Explanation                |
| ------------------ | -------------------------- |
| Initialization     | Assign initial values      |
| Object consistency | Enforce mandatory data     |
| Encapsulation      | Controlled object creation |
| Immutability       | Initialize final fields    |

  3. Types of Constructors in Java
Java mainly supports:
  Default Constructor
  No-Argument Constructor
  Parameterized Constructor

1. Default Constructor

Provided by the compiler only if no constructor is defined.

  class Employee {
      int id;
      String name;
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          Employee e = new Employee();
          System.out.println(e.id);
          System.out.println(e.name);
      }
  }
  
  Output
  0
  null
⚠️ If you define any constructor, compiler will NOT create default constructor

2. No-Argument Constructor (User-Defined)
  class Employee {
      Employee() {
          System.out.println("No-arg constructor called");
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new Employee();
      }
  }
  
  Output
  No-arg constructor called

3. Parameterized Constructor
    class Employee {
        int id;
        String name;
    
        Employee(int id, String name) {
            this.id = id;
            this.name = name;
        }
    }
    
    public class Test {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Employee e = new Employee(101, "Sai");
            System.out.println(e.id + " " + e.name);
        }
    }
    
    Output
    101 Sai

4. Constructor Overloading
  Multiple constructors with different parameter lists.

  class Product {
      Product() {
          System.out.println("Default constructor");
      }
  
      Product(int id) {
          System.out.println("Product ID: " + id);
      }
  
      Product(int id, String name) {
          System.out.println(id + " " + name);
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new Product();
          new Product(1);
          new Product(2, "Laptop");
      }
  }
  
  Output:
  Default constructor
  Product ID: 1
  2 Laptop

5. Constructor Chaining
  Using this()
  Used to call another constructor of same class
  Must be first statement

  class Demo {
      Demo() {
          this(10);
          System.out.println("Default constructor");
      }
  
      Demo(int x) {
          System.out.println("Parameterized constructor: " + x);
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new Demo();
      }
  }
  
  Output
  Parameterized constructor: 10
  Default constructor

Using super():
Calls parent class constructor
If not written, compiler adds super() automatically
    
    class Parent {
        Parent() {
            System.out.println("Parent constructor");
        }
    }
    
    class Child extends Parent {
        Child() {
            super();
            System.out.println("Child constructor");
        }
    }
    
    public class Test {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            new Child();
        }
    }
    
    Output
    Parent constructor
    Child constructor

6. Constructor Execution Order
  Parent class constructor
  Child class constructor

  class A {
      A() {
          System.out.println("A");
      }
  }
  
  class B extends A {
      B() {
          System.out.println("B");
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new B();
      }
  }
  
  Output
  A
  B

8. Constructor vs Method
| Feature     | Constructor       | Method         |
| ----------- | ----------------- | -------------- |
| Name        | Same as class     | Any name       |
| Return Type | No                | Yes            |
| Invocation  | Automatic         | Manual         |
| Purpose     | Initialize object | Business logic |
| Inheritance | Not inherited     | Inherited      |

9. Can Constructors Be Overridden?
❌ No — constructors are not inherited
But you can call parent constructor using super()

10. Can Constructor Be final or static?
❌ No
Why?
static: belongs to class, constructor belongs to object
final: constructor cannot be inherited anyway 

11. Exception Handling in Constructors
class Test {
    Test() throws Exception {
        System.out.println("Constructor");
    }
}


✔ Allowed
✔ Common in file, DB initialization

12. Spring Boot Perspective (Important for 4+ yrs exp)
Constructor Injection (Recommended)
@Service
class UserService {

    private final UserRepository repo;

    public UserService(UserRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}

Benefits
  Immutability
  Easier testing
  Avoids circular dependency
  Preferred over field injection

13. Interview Tricky Questions
Q1. What happens if no constructor is defined?
➡ Compiler provides default constructor.

Q2. Can constructor call another constructor?
➡ Yes, using this() or super().

Q3. Can we create object without constructor?
➡ ❌ No (Reflection is exception – advanced topic)

Q4. Why this() must be first statement?
➡ To ensure proper object initialization order.

Q5. Is constructor inherited?
➡ ❌ No
  
Q6. Difference between default & no-arg constructor?
  | Default                           | No-Arg             |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------ |
| Compiler provided                 | Developer provided |
| Removed if any constructor exists | Always available   |

  
